Great questions! Here's the answers I have: 



 1. Insurance integration is very attractive - do you have a button within Pearl to send images to insurance companies to get immediate approval? If not, is it in the workings for Softdent/Carestream users? 

 &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; We don't have that functionality&nbsp;available&nbsp;yet, but it will be coming for our partners (which includes Carestream)  


 2. When viewing a PAN or CBCT through Pearl, does it highlight findings such as cysts, tumors or lesions? If not, is this something that the FDA may approve in the future or won't they ever for this type of thing in a dental office? 
 &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; 
 Yes, when we release CBCT/3D imaging with Pearl, those&nbsp;will be just a few of the many pathologies and findings that we detect.&nbsp;  


 3. Does the PDF export/save include stats/narrative? Can you please send us an example of this to show our team too? (or is this what the Ryan Adamson Oct 8, 2025&nbsp;image that you attached?) 
 &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; 
 The Ryan&nbsp;Adamson document is an example of what the&nbsp;PDF export will include. Is there something specific you're wondering that it will show?&nbsp;  


 4. The % amount of the tooth that is compromised that is shown on the tooth - is there another % breakdown within that that can be seen? (ie % of previous filling&nbsp;+ decay&nbsp;+ cavity)? 
 &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; 
 Right now, it only shows the overall percentage that's affected. Updates will be coming out where it will give a deeper breakdown.  


 5. Is there something that will alert when a patient is due for certain xrays? Could we set the parameters? Or would it be smart enough to sync with their insurance? 
 &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; 
 So we offer that functionality; however, it only integrates with Open Dental, Dentrix, and Eaglesoft practice softwares at the moment.&nbsp;  


 6. What is the accuracy rate of the metrics shown on this program?&nbsp; 
 &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; 
 Based on several studies from universities last year (including UCLA), our accuracy rate is 95%.&nbsp;  


 Just curious if most insurance companies use Overjet to look at % of compromised teeth, bone loss&nbsp;#s, etc - would the numbers that Pearl is finding be the same? How far off are they? 
 &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; 
 They would be very similar if not the same.

